  "summary": "New print movement combining traditional ukiyo-e techniques with Western-style perspective and lighting.",
  "description": "Revitalized ukiyo-e with modern subjects, often produced through collaborative efforts between artists, carvers, printers, and publishers.",
  "culturalContext": "Shin-hanga (\"new prints\", 1915-1940s) — Watanabe Shōzaburō's publisher-driven revival — reengineered ukiyo-e for export and modern Japan: Hasui Kawase's snow-and-night landscapes, Itō Shinsui's beauties, Yamamura Kōka's Noh portraits, Yoshida Hiroshi's travel prints. The system (artist-publisher-carver-printer) produced subtler bokashi gradients than Edo woodblock; American collections (Robert Muller's Toledo trove) canonized it, and Hasui's \"travel peace\" imagery is Japan's best-loved print idiom after Hiroshige.",
